<!--
    A pointer the binary stores to a global and keeps using from its register
    is dereferenced through its own variable, not through the global (P3
    kuna_pointeestorekeep, P6 kuna_pointeevalue; GitHub issue #767).

    Merge joins a register value with the global it is copied to.  The joined
    value then prints as a read of the global, and a dereference through it
    takes the global's pointee type.  For

        char *gc;
        int p_int(int *p, int k) { int *q = p + k; gc = (char *)q; return q[1] + q[2]; }

    kuna used to print `gc = &a0[a1]; return gc[2] + gc[1];`.  kuna never
    declares the global, so a reader who gives `gc` its real type reads two
    bytes and gets 0 where the binary returns 9; a `struct rec *` global made
    `return gr[3] + *gr;` add two records, and a `void *` global made
    `gv[1] * 3` not compile.  RulePropagateCopy keeps the store's COPY where the
    binary makes it when a marker would take it, and Merge refuses the
    copy-shadow join, so the value keeps its own variable and type.  A load of
    the global the binary makes after the store stays a load: g_alias stores
    through a pointer that may point at gi before reading gi[1], and kuna used
    to print `return (&a0[a1])[1];`.  g_reload, g_before, g_write, g_diff,
    g_twice, g_old and g_branch also read the value from its register; a load
    of the global after a store through a pointer still reads the global,
    g_twice keeps the value it loaded between two such stores in a variable of
    its own, g_old still reads the global's earlier value, and g_branch stores
    in both arms of a branch.

    Every assertion below fails on the unfixed tree, and #9 to #15 fail on a
    tree that keeps the value apart without keeping those loads.  The wrapper only supplies
    the harness architecture (`load file` needs one).  The pass loads
    globalpointee_gcc_O2_x86_64 and globalpointee_gcc_O0_x86_64 (built from
    globalpointee_x86_64.c beside them).
    At -O2 the binary loads q[1] and q[2] before it stores q to the global,
    and those loads keep variables of their own ahead of the store
    (`option indexaliasguard global`, issue #792): #3 and #4 check the -O0
    build, #16 and #17 the -O2 one.
-->
